# why

A Linux program troubleshooter.

```bash
why ./some-broken-program
```

and it answers:

```text
WHY DOES THIS PROGRAM NOT WORK?

✗ ELF interpreter
  /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2        OK

✗ Shared libraries
  libwebkit2gtk-4.0.so.37            MISSING

  Required by: /usr/bin/BitComet
  Package providing it: ???

✗ Symbol dependencies
  3 unresolved symbols

✓ Architecture
  x86-64

✓ GPU
  AMD Radeon RX ...

✓ Vulkan
  Vulkan 1.4 ...

✓ Wayland
  KDE Plasma / KWin

⚠ Environment
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH is set

Suggested next steps:
  ...
```

And it could get progressively more ambitious.

```bash
why ./game
why --steam 123456
why --flatpak com.example.Game
why --wine game.exe
why --crash firefox
```

It could inspect:

* ELF headers and dynamic loader
* shared libraries and symbol versions
* package ownership
* ABI mismatches
* permissions
* Wayland/X11
* Vulkan/OpenGL
* GPU/driver state
* Flatpak sandboxing
* Wine/Proton
* environment variables
* coredumps
* relevant `journalctl` messages
* recent kernel errors
* filesystem / mount problems

And then produce a **human-readable explanation rather than 800 lines of diagnostic output**.

That last part is important.

Linux already has an absurd collection of excellent tools. `ldd`, `readelf`, `strace`, `coredumpctl`, `journalctl`, `vulkaninfo`, MangoHud, `wayland-debug`, etc. For example, `wayland-debug` can already inspect and filter Wayland protocol traffic, while MangoHud can log FPS, frame timing, GPU/CPU telemetry, and more. ([GitHub][2])

The missing layer is often:

> **“Okay, but what the fuck does all of this mean?”**

That is why the project exists

---

# Why would someone use it

The classic linux problems :

```text
program doesn't launch
       ↓
missing library
       ↓
package changed
       ↓
wrong ABI
```

GPU:

```text
game stutters
       ↓
VRAM?
GPU saturation?
shader compilation?
frame pacing?
Wayland?
Wine?
kernel?
driver?
```

Networking:

```text
website doesn't load
       ↓
DNS?
DoH?
proxy?
VPN?
TLS?
routing?
```

Audio:

```text
program renders incorrectly
       ↓
PipeWire?
WirePlumber?
ALSA?
JACK?
```

Instead of manually doing:

```bash
journalctl ...
grep ...
ldd ...
readelf ...
pacman -Qo ...
vulkaninfo ...
ps ...
cat /proc/...
```

the program becomes the thing that orchestrates all of that.

---

## "bug report mode"

```bash
why --report ./broken-program
```

Output:

```text
WHY 0.4.1

Application
  Path: /usr/bin/foo
  PID: 19382
  Version: 4.2.1

System
  Arch Linux
  Linux 7.x
  KDE Plasma 6.x
  Wayland
  AMD GPU

Problem detected
  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  libfoo.so.3 requires GLIBC_2.42
  system provides GLIBC_2.41

Confidence: 99%

Relevant logs
  ...

Possible fixes
  1. Update package foo
  2. Reinstall package foo
  3. Check for partial system upgrade

Diagnostic bundle:
  /tmp/why-report-19382.tar.zst
```

Then someone on Reddit/Discord/GitHub can literally say:

> Run `why --report`, paste the output.

That is the point where a hobby project starts turning into infrastructure.


## Probably a evolution path??
```text
v0.1
ELF + shared libraries

v0.2
Arch package ownership

v0.3
process/environment inspection

v0.4
Wayland/X11/Vulkan

v0.5
Flatpak

v0.6
Wine/Proton

v0.7
kernel/journal/coredumps

v0.8
automatic diagnosis

v0.9
HTML reports

v1.0
"paste this output into your GitHub issue"
```

